  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 301 PM MDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nThe Long term forecast is pretty complex. Saturday \nmorning a weak shortwave that's going to push through the western \nportion of Wyoming giving a chance for some morning to early \nafternoon showers for the southwestern corner of Carbon county. \nEverywhere else in the forecast area will see dry conditions and \ntemperatures in the upper 50's to low 60's. Around trick or treating \ntime most of the area will be hovering around 45 degrees for some \npossible light jacket conditions with the light winds. \n\nSunday into early next week,\n\nA complex storm system will push across the western CONUS Sunday \ninto early next week. Two major players will make up this system: \nfirst, a strong, moisture-laden upper level low riding the \nsubtropical jet stream into the southwest and second, a potent \nPacific trough pushing into the northwest. The primary questions \nconcerning the evolution of this system are if, when, and where the \ntwo approaching systems might merge together. The model guidance has \na hard time resolving this event often flip flopping how cold the \nair aloft will be and how much forcing will be present during the \nnext few days. Latest probabilities are a 50 percent chance for 5 \ninches of snow concentrated on our mountain peaks and adjacent areas \nabove 9,000t and possibly 1-3 inches for areas like Rawlins and \nLaramie as they sit at roughly 7,000ft. Then for areas for along and \neast of the I-25 corridor the 50 percent chance is for an inch or \nless as it may be too warm most of the time for adequate snow \naccumulation. The 90th percentile which would favor colder \ntemperatures aloft and an earlier merger has about 10 inches for our \npeaks, 3-5 inches for Laramie and Rawlins and 1-2 inches of snow \nalong the I-25 corridor with trace to an inch about as far east as \nKimball Nebraska. Some of the major news networks have already begun \nposting the clickbait potential focusing on the very upper end of \nthe accumulation amounts. But if the air aloft stays between 3-6C \ninstead of diving into the negatives then we are more likely to end \nup with a decent amount of rain and very little snow Sunday through \nTuesday. The 10 percentile encompasses this scenario a little better \nand the snow accumulation becomes 1-2 inches for our mountain tops \nand a trace amount for Laramie and Rawlins. For areas along and east \nof I-25 the precipitation would most likely stay as rain for \nmajority of the time and have a rain/snow mix during the heavier \nrain portions. The high-resolution models take their first stab at \nthe system soon and start to resolve the system and throw their \nweight towards the most likely scenario.",
